//! What the datasheets of the catalog rows of the standard generics say about layout (issue
//! #1032).
//!
//! A generic such as `LinearRegulator` picks a catalog row, and each row has its own datasheet,
//! so the guidelines are not the generic's. One block for each row that has them, keyed by the
//! row's manufacturer's part number (`mpn` of its catalog file):
//!
//! ```text
//! layout "XC6206P332MR-G" for LinearRegulator {
//!     source "document, section, p.N"
//!     near input_cap bridging .vin.pos .vin.neg to .vin.pos
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! The statements are those of a part's `layout` block and name the generic's pins; a board
//! that picks the row gets the block's guidelines in its design, and a board that picks
//! another row gets none of them. A row has one package, so its block is for that package.
//!
//! A row has no block when its datasheet has no layout section, or when it could not be read;
//! `data/jlcpcb/README.md` lists each such row and says which. A statement has a number only
//! where the datasheet gives one, and every block has the document, the section and the page
//! as its `source`.
